# S3 Files Conventions

These rules encode knowledge gaps in the AWS docs and constraints that apply throughout every page in this repo.

## CLI namespace

S3 Files uses a **separate CLI service namespace** — not `aws s3` or `aws efs`. **Minimum: AWS CLI v2.34.26.** Older v2 (e.g. 2.34.23) and AWS CLI v1 return `Found invalid choice 's3files'`.

Document the minimum CLI in the setup index **Prerequisites** section (`src/content/docs/setup/index.mdx`).

```bash frame="terminal"
# ✅ Correct (S3 Files uses list-* / get-* for reads, not describe-*)
aws s3files create-file-system ...
aws s3files list-file-systems ...
aws s3files get-file-system ...
aws s3files create-mount-target ...
aws s3files list-mount-targets ...
aws s3files create-access-point ...
aws s3files get-access-point ...

# ❌ Wrong
aws s3 create-file-system ...
aws efs create-file-system ...
aws s3files describe-file-systems ...   # operation does not exist
```

## Mount type

The mount helper registers a new filesystem type `s3files`. Always use `-t s3files`, not `-t nfs4`.

```bash frame="terminal"
# ✅ Correct
sudo mount -t s3files $FS_ID:/ /mnt/s3files

# ❌ Wrong
sudo mount -t nfs4 ...
```

## Two IAM roles — always document both

Every setup or IAM page must explain **both** roles side-by-side:

| Role | Who assumes it | Purpose |
| ---- | -------------- | ------- |
| **File system role** | `elasticfilesystem.amazonaws.com` | S3 Files service reads/writes the linked S3 bucket and manages EventBridge sync rules |
| **Compute role** | EC2 instance profile / ECS task role / Lambda exec role / EKS IRSA or Pod Identity | Compute mounts the file system and accesses objects directly |

The service principal is `elasticfilesystem.amazonaws.com` (not `s3.amazonaws.com`) — this surprises readers; call it out explicitly.

In the **trust policy**, `aws:SourceArn` must be `arn:aws:s3files:REGION:ACCOUNT:file-system/*`, **not** `arn:aws:elasticfilesystem:…`. Wrong `SourceArn` breaks assume-role/sync and can show **Unknown Error** on console Synchronization. Inline policy EventBridge manage actions must use `DO-NOT-DELETE-S3-Files*` with `events:ManagedBy` per [AWS prerequisites](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-files-prereq-policies.html). User-facing detail lives on [File System](/s3-files-workloads/setup/filesystem/).

## Bucket prerequisites (must appear before any setup steps)

Always check these before creating the file system:

1. **Versioning must be enabled** — S3 Files requires versioning for sync.
2. **Only SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS** — SSE-C and DSSE-KMS are not supported.

```bash frame="terminal"
aws s3api put-bucket-versioning \
  --bucket $BUCKET \
  --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled

aws s3api get-bucket-encryption --bucket $BUCKET
```

## EC2: `amazon-efs-utils` version requirement

The mount helper is shipped as part of `amazon-efs-utils`. S3 Files requires **version 3.0.0 or above**.

```bash frame="terminal"
# Amazon Linux 2023
sudo dnf install -y amazon-efs-utils

# Amazon Linux 2 / generic
sudo yum install -y amazon-efs-utils

# Ubuntu / Debian
curl https://amazon-efs-utils.aws.com/efs-utils-installer.sh | sudo sh -s -- --install
```

## ECS: Fargate and Managed Instances only

**S3 Files volumes are NOT supported on the ECS EC2 launch type.** If a task definition with an S3 Files volume is run on EC2 launch type, the task will fail. Always include this callout at the top of ECS pages:

```mdx
<Aside type="caution">
  S3 Files volumes work on **Fargate** and **ECS Managed Instances** only.
  The EC2 launch type is not supported — tasks will fail if configured with an S3 Files volume and run on EC2.
</Aside>
```

## Lambda: access point required

Lambda requires an **access point** to mount an S3 file system — it cannot mount a file system directly by ID. When auto-created by the console, the access point uses UID/GID `1000/1000` and a `/Lambda` scope.

Always create the access point via CLI on the Lambda pages:

```bash frame="terminal"
aws s3files create-access-point \
  --file-system-id $FS_ID \
  --posix-user Uid=1000,Gid=1000 \
  --root-directory Path=/lambda \
  --region $AWS_REGION
```

## EKS: EFS CSI driver is the same driver

S3 Files on EKS uses the **Amazon EFS CSI driver** (`aws-efs-csi-driver`) — not a separate driver. The same Helm chart and StorageClass serve both EFS and S3 Files. Always note this in EKS pages so readers know they are not installing something new.

## Security group port

S3 Files uses NFS over port **2049 TCP**. Always show the explicit `aws ec2 authorize-security-group-*` commands, not just a table:

**`create-security-group --description`:** EC2 accepts **ASCII only** in the description string. Do not use typographic dashes (`—`, `–`) or other Unicode in CLI examples; AWS returns `InvalidParameterValue` / “Character sets beyond ASCII are not supported.”

```bash frame="terminal"
# Outbound from compute SG to mount target SG
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-egress \
  --group-id $COMPUTE_SG_ID \
  --protocol tcp --port 2049 \
  --source-group $MOUNT_TARGET_SG_ID \
  --region $AWS_REGION

# Inbound on mount target SG from compute SG
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id $MOUNT_TARGET_SG_ID \
  --protocol tcp --port 2049 \
  --source-group $COMPUTE_SG_ID \
  --region $AWS_REGION
```

## Standard export block

Every CLI walkthrough page must start with a consistent export block so readers can copy-paste the rest of the commands:

```bash frame="terminal"
export AWS_REGION=ap-southeast-6
export BUCKET=my-s3-files-bucket
export BUCKET_ARN=arn:aws:s3:::${BUCKET}
export ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)
export FS_ID=fs-0123456789abcdef0   # set after create-file-system
```

